{% trace-scenario title="Trust the score, or check the mechanism?" prompt="A team's LLM judge has scored 'helpfulness' across two release cycles. The number has climbed steadily. Should the next release lean on that trend?" outcome="Not without calibration. The judge turned out to reward answer length, not correctness — a well-documented verbosity bias nobody checked because the score was never compared against human judgment." /%}

## At a glance

A team adopts an LLM judge to score "helpfulness" across thousands of production runs. The aggregate score climbs for two release cycles, so the team treats it as evidence the product is improving. Then someone finally samples the judge's transcripts against human reviewers and finds the pattern: the judge was consistently rating longer answers higher, independent of whether those answers were correct. The score had been measuring verbosity, not helpfulness, and nobody caught it because the judge's output was never checked against a human baseline.

**LLM-as-a-judge means using one model to score another model's output against a rubric.** It is a useful, {% highlight %}cost-effective way to evaluate criteria that are too subjective or too expensive for a fixed rule{% /highlight %}, but it is not ground truth. It works reasonably well for criteria with a clear, describable rubric — tone, format adherence, relative preference between two answers. It works poorly for criteria that need a verified fact, a precise count, or resistance to a judge's own biases toward length, position, or its own outputs. Those need a deterministic check or a human reviewer instead. This guide is for engineers and applied-AI practitioners deciding which evaluation criteria belong in which lane, and how to keep a judge honest once it is running in production.

This article is scoped narrowly to judge *methodology*: when to trust an LLM judge, its known failure modes, and how to calibrate it. It does not re-cover general evaluation metric taxonomy — see [AI evaluation metrics](/blog/ai-evaluation-metrics) for that — or dataset construction. If you have not yet decided what to measure at all, start there and come back here once you know which criteria you plan to hand to a model.

### The Three-Layer Evaluation Routing Model

Every evaluation criterion should be routed to exactly one of three layers: a **deterministic check**, an **LLM judge**, or **human review**. The routing decision comes from two questions, asked in order:

1. **Can this be checked with fixed logic?** If yes, route it to a deterministic check. Do not spend a model call — or a person's attention — on something a regex, a schema validator, or a lookup can already verify.
2. **Does it require subjective or comparative judgment that a rubric can describe?** If yes, and the criterion is not high-stakes or fact-dependent, route it to an LLM judge with a written rubric. If the judgment is high-stakes, requires verified facts, or the judge's agreement with humans hasn't been established, route it to human review.

| Criterion type | Can fixed logic check it? | Needs describable subjective judgment? | Route | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Format and schema compliance | Yes | — | Deterministic | Output matches required JSON schema |
| Policy and safety hard blocks | Yes | — | Deterministic | Forbidden tool never called |
| Exact factual lookups | Yes (if source is structured) | — | Deterministic | Refund amount matches order record |
| Tone and style adherence | No | Yes, describable | LLM judge | Reply matches brand voice guidelines |
| Relative preference between two answers | No | Yes, describable | LLM judge | Which of two drafts reads more clearly |
| Rubric-scored helpfulness (calibrated) | No | Yes, describable | LLM judge | Did the answer address the stated need |
| Open-ended factual correctness | No | Partially — needs ground truth | Human review | Is this medical or legal claim accurate |
| High-consequence or irreversible actions | No | No — needs accountability | Human review | Approve a large refund or account closure |
| Judge-human disagreement cases | No | Unresolved | Human review | Calibration sample flags low agreement |

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Operating principle" title="A judge score is an estimate, not a verdict." body="Treat an LLM judge the way you'd treat a fast, cheap, imperfect reviewer: useful at scale, wrong often enough that it needs a calibration process and an escalation path, never the final word on a high-stakes case." tone="yellow" /%}

## What LLM-as-a-judge means and doesn't mean

LLM-as-a-judge is a specific technique: you write a rubric, hand it and a candidate output (sometimes alongside a reference or a second candidate for comparison) to a model, and ask it to produce a score or a preference along with a justification. It is not a certification that the scored output is correct. It is not a substitute for ground truth. And it is not free of the same failure modes — hallucination, inconsistency, sensitivity to phrasing — that affect the model being judged.

The appeal is obvious: human review does not scale to thousands of production runs a day, and deterministic checks cannot describe "does this response sound appropriately empathetic" or "is this the better of two summaries." An LLM judge fills that gap at a fraction of the cost of a human panel, and it can run continuously rather than on a sampled cadence.

The risk is just as obvious once you've seen it happen: a judge score can look stable, trustworthy, and improving while measuring something other than what you think it measures. The opening scenario — a verbosity-biased judge quietly rewarding longer answers for two release cycles — is not a contrived edge case. It is the default outcome of deploying a judge without ever checking its agreement with a human baseline.

### The two routing questions, applied

Before writing a rubric for anything, ask the two questions from the Three-Layer Model above.

**"Can this be checked with fixed logic?"** covers more ground than teams usually assume. Schema validity, required fields, forbidden actions, numeric ranges, and lookups against a structured source of truth are all deterministic by nature — a judge model adds latency, cost, and a new source of error to a check that a script already answers with certainty. If a criterion can be reduced to a boolean or a lookup, it should never reach a judge.

**"Does this require subjective or comparative judgment that a rubric can describe?"** is where the judge layer earns its place. Tone, clarity, adherence to a style guide, and relative preference between two candidate answers are all criteria a written rubric can describe precisely enough for a model to apply consistently — not perfectly, but consistently enough to be useful once calibrated. Criteria that fail this test — because they require verified facts the judge cannot check, or because the judgment is too high-stakes to delegate — belong in human review instead.

## What LLM judges are reasonably good at

Used inside their actual competence, LLM judges are a legitimate part of an evaluation system. They tend to hold up well for:

- **Relative preference between two answers.** "Which of these two responses better addresses the user's question" is a comparison a judge can make more reliably than an absolute score, because it removes the need to anchor to an abstract scale.
- **Style and tone adherence.** Whether a response matches a brand voice, a required formality level, or a communication policy is a describable, rubric-friendly judgment.
- **Rubric-scored dimensions with clear criteria.** When a rubric spells out exactly what counts as a 1 versus a 5, with concrete examples at each point, a judge can apply it more consistently than it can invent a definition of "good" on the fly.
- **High-volume triage.** Flagging the subset of runs that look risky, off-tone, or worth a closer look, so that human attention goes to the highest-value cases instead of a random sample.

They are considerably less reliable at anything that requires verifying a fact against the world, counting precisely, resisting surface features of the text (like length or formatting) that correlate with but do not cause quality, or judging its own or a closely related model's output without a documented tendency toward self-preference. [AI agent evaluation](/blog/ai-agent-evaluation-practical-guide) covers where judges fit inside a broader evaluation program, alongside deterministic checks and regression suites.

## Known judge failure modes

Every documented judge bias below is a *tendency*, not a guaranteed failure on every run. {% highlight %}None of these biases are eliminated by a mitigation — they are reduced{% /highlight %}, and the residual risk should stay visible in how you interpret the score.

| Bias type | Description | Mitigation (reduces, does not eliminate) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Position bias | The judge favors whichever answer is shown first (or second) in a comparison, regardless of quality | Randomize answer order across evaluations; run each comparison both ways and check for order-flip disagreement |
| Verbosity bias | The judge rates longer answers as better independent of correctness or actual usefulness | Instruct the rubric to explicitly penalize unnecessary length; calibrate against human judgments across a length-varied sample; report length alongside the score |
| Self-preference / agreement bias | A judge model rates outputs from the same model family, or outputs that match its own stylistic habits, more favorably | Use a judge model from a different family or lineage than the model being evaluated where practical; track agreement rates by model pairing |
| Rubric drift | The judge's effective interpretation of the rubric shifts over time as the model, prompt, or rubric wording changes, even when the written rubric hasn't changed | Version the rubric, prompt, and judge model together; re-run a fixed calibration set on every change and compare score distributions |

### Position bias

A judge asked to compare Answer A and Answer B will, in a documented pattern across LLM-judge research, show a measurable lean toward one position in the prompt — sometimes the first answer, sometimes the second, depending on the model and prompt structure. This is why any comparative judge setup should randomize which answer appears first, and ideally run the comparison in both orders to see whether the verdict flips. {% highlight %}A verdict that flips with position order is not a reliable verdict{% /highlight %}; it is noise dressed as a score.

### Verbosity bias

This is the failure mode from the opening scenario, and it deserves emphasis because it is easy to introduce accidentally. A judge instructed to score "helpfulness" or "completeness" without an explicit length constraint will often reward the longer of two answers, because longer answers *look* more thorough even when they pad with restatement, hedging, or tangential detail. The mitigation is not to cap length arbitrarily — that trades one bias for another — but to write the rubric so it explicitly separates thoroughness from length, and to calibrate the judge against human scores across a sample that varies deliberately in length so the bias becomes visible if it's still present.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Illustrative transcript" title="Same content, different length, same judge" body="Rubric: score helpfulness 1–5. Answer A (46 words) states the correct fix and stops. Answer B (210 words) states the same fix, then adds three paragraphs of general context the user didn't ask for. An uncalibrated judge scores B a 5 and A a 3, citing 'thoroughness.' A human reviewer scores both a 4 — B's extra length didn't add correctness, only length. This is the pattern a calibration sample is built to catch." tone="dark" /%}

![A black canvas showing two answer transcripts side by side, a short 46-word correct answer scored 3 out of 5 by an LLM judge, and a 210-word padded answer scored 5 out of 5 by the same judge, with a human reviewer's scores of 4 and 4 shown beneath both](/media/llm-as-a-judge-evaluation-transcript.svg)

### Self-preference / agreement bias

When the judge model and the model being evaluated share a lineage — the same provider, the same base model, or even the same model at a different checkpoint — some evaluations show the judge favoring outputs that match its own stylistic tendencies. This does not mean judge and candidate must always come from different vendors; it means {% highlight %}the possibility of self-preference should be treated as a risk to check for, not assumed absent{% /highlight %}. Comparing agreement rates when the judge and candidate are matched versus mismatched is one way to surface whether this is happening in your setup.

### Rubric drift

A rubric is text, and text is interpreted differently by different model versions, different prompt phrasings around it, and even the same model at different points if the underlying provider updates it silently. Rubric drift is what happens when the *written* rubric stays the same but the judge's *effective* behavior under it changes — scores creep up, down, or become less consistent without anyone editing the rubric file. The only reliable way to catch this is to keep a fixed calibration set and re-run it whenever the judge model, prompt, or rubric changes, comparing the new score distribution against the old one.

## Writing a rubric a judge can actually follow

A rubric that works for a human reviewer is not automatically a rubric that works for a model. Vague criteria like "score the response's quality from 1 to 10" invite exactly the biases above, because the model has nothing concrete to anchor to and will fall back on surface heuristics like length and confidence. A rubric a judge can actually follow needs:

- **Named criteria**, each scored separately rather than folded into one composite number.
- **A bounded scale** with a concrete description of what separates each point on the scale — not just "1 = bad, 5 = good."
- **A required justification field**, so the judge has to point to specific evidence in the output rather than emitting a bare number. This also gives a human reviewer something to check during calibration.
- **Explicit counter-instructions against known biases** — for example, telling the judge directly that length alone should not raise the score, and that answer order in a comparison should not influence the verdict.

### Illustrative rubric-as-structured-prompt example

The following is illustrative — adapt the criteria and scale to your actual product and rubric, and verify the prompt format against your model provider's current tool-use or structured-output conventions before using it.

```text
# Illustrative — verify against your provider's structured-output conventions before use.

You are scoring a single customer support response against the rubric below.
Do not favor a longer response unless the extra length adds information the
user needs. Base every score on the response text only, not on how
confident or fluent it sounds.

## Criteria (score each 1-5 independently)

1. correctness — Does the response accurately reflect the account/order
   data provided in context? 1 = contradicts the data, 5 = fully consistent.
2. completeness — Does it address every part of the user's stated request?
   1 = ignores parts of the request, 5 = addresses all of it, no more.
3. tone — Does it match the brand's support tone (direct, respectful,
   no over-apologizing)? 1 = off-tone, 5 = on-tone.
4. actionability — If the user needs to do something next, is it stated
   clearly? 1 = unclear or missing, 5 = a single unambiguous next step.

## Required output (JSON)

{
  "correctness": <1-5>,
  "completeness": <1-5>,
  "tone": <1-5>,
  "actionability": <1-5>,
  "justification": "<cite the specific sentence(s) in the response that
    drove each score below its expected value; state 'none' if all
    scores matched expectation>"
}
```

The `justification` field is not decoration. {% highlight %}It is what makes calibration possible{% /highlight %}: a human reviewer can read the justification against the actual response and judge whether the model's reasoning holds up, rather than only comparing two numbers.

## Calibrating a judge against human review

A judge that has never been compared against a human baseline is a judge you are trusting on faith. Calibration is the process of checking that faith periodically and honestly.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Diagnostic shortcut" title="Calibrate before you trust, not after you're surprised." body="Run the calibration sample before the judge goes anywhere near a release gate. If you only calibrate after a score looks suspicious, you've already been making decisions on an unverified number." tone="dark" /%}

### Calibration-process checklist

- [ ] **Sample size.** Pull a sample large enough to see the failure modes that matter for your use case — a small, curated sample that deliberately includes length-varied, order-varied, and edge-case examples is more useful than a larger but uniform random sample. Report the sample size and how it was selected alongside every calibration result.
- [ ] **Agreement threshold.** Decide, before looking at results, what level of judge-human agreement is acceptable for this criterion to stay routed to the judge layer. There is no universal number to cite here — the threshold should reflect how costly a judge error is for that specific criterion, and it should be written down before the calibration run so it can't drift to fit an inconvenient result.
- [ ] **Disagreement classification.** When the judge and a human reviewer disagree, classify why: ambiguous rubric, missing context the judge didn't have, a genuine judge error, or a human reviewer error. Not all disagreement means the judge is wrong.
- [ ] **Re-calibration cadence.** Re-run the fixed calibration set whenever the judge model, prompt, or rubric changes, and on a regular schedule even when nothing has changed, since provider-side model updates can shift behavior silently. Tie the cadence to your release cadence, not to a fixed calendar interval that might miss an intermediate change.
- [ ] **Versioned record.** Keep the judge model version, rubric version, calibration sample version, and agreement result together as one artifact, so a later reviewer can tell which judge configuration a historical score actually came from.

> **Decision rule:** If judge-human agreement on the calibration sample falls below the threshold you set in advance, move that criterion to human review — or narrow the rubric and recalibrate — before trusting the judge's score in a release decision again.

## Confidence thresholds and escalation to a human

Even a calibrated judge should not resolve every case on its own. A practical pattern is to let the judge's own output signal when it is uncertain — a low or borderline score, a justification that hedges, or a case that falls in a category the calibration sample showed disagreement on — and route those cases to a human reviewer rather than accepting the judge's verdict automatically. This keeps the volume advantage of an LLM judge (most cases never need a human) while keeping the accountability advantage of human review for the cases where it matters most. [Human-in-the-loop review](/blog/human-in-the-loop-ai-agents) covers how to design that escalation path so it doesn't become a bottleneck or a rubber stamp.

Borderline-score escalation is a mitigation, not a guarantee: {% highlight %}a confidently wrong judge score will not trigger escalation{% /highlight %}, which is exactly why the calibration sample — not the live confidence signal alone — is what tells you whether the judge deserves this much trust in the first place.

