{% rubric-selector-option label="Factual accuracy (open-ended)" layer="Human review" fixedLogic="No — the claim isn't checkable against a structured source the system has access to." subjectiveJudgment="Partially — judging accuracy needs verified ground truth, not just a describable rubric." reason="A judge can flag a claim as suspicious, but confirming it as true or false against the world is a human (or a verified-source deterministic check, if one exists) job, not a rubric-following one." /%}
{% rubric-selector-option label="Tone / brand voice adherence" layer="LLM judge" fixedLogic="No — tone isn't reducible to a fixed rule." subjectiveJudgment="Yes — a style guide with examples is exactly the kind of rubric a judge can apply consistently." reason="Tone adherence is subjective but describable: give the judge concrete examples of on-tone and off-tone responses and it can apply the distinction at scale." /%}
{% rubric-selector-option label="Format / schema compliance" layer="Deterministic" fixedLogic="Yes — a schema validator or regex can check this with certainty." subjectiveJudgment="Not relevant — no judgment is needed once fixed logic can answer it." reason="Never spend a model call on something a validator already answers exactly. Route it to code, not a judge." /%}
{% rubric-selector-option label="Relative preference between two answers" layer="LLM judge" fixedLogic="No — preference between two texts isn't a fixed-logic question." subjectiveJudgment="Yes — comparative preference is describable and judges tend to be more reliable here than on absolute scores." reason="Comparative judgments avoid the anchoring problem of absolute scoring, but still need position-bias mitigation: randomize order and check for order-flip disagreement." /%}
{% rubric-selector-option label="High-consequence account action" layer="Human review" fixedLogic="Partially — some preconditions may be deterministic, but the decision to act isn't." subjectiveJudgment="No — the issue isn't judgment quality, it's accountability for an irreversible action." reason="Irreversible or high-stakes actions stay with a human regardless of how well-calibrated the judge is, because the cost of a rare judge error is too high to delegate." /%}
{% /rubric-selector %}

