## Worked example: judging a support-agent response for tone and policy adherence

A support team wants to evaluate whether their agent's responses stay on-brand and inside policy across thousands of daily conversations. Running every response past a human reviewer isn't feasible at that volume, so they route the criteria using the two questions.

**Policy hard blocks** — did the agent avoid offering unauthorized discounts, avoid confirming account changes without verification, and avoid disclosing internal policy details — are all checkable with fixed logic against the conversation transcript and the agent's tool calls. These go to deterministic checks: a rule engine that flags any response containing a disallowed pattern or an unauthorized tool call. No judge call is spent here.

**Tone adherence** — does the response sound direct and respectful rather than over-apologetic or curt — is subjective but describable. The team writes a rubric with three or four concrete examples of on-tone and off-tone responses at each score level, adds a required justification field, and routes this to an LLM judge.

**Escalation-worthy edge cases** — a customer expressing genuine distress, an ambiguous refund request near a policy boundary, or any case where the judge's score falls in the lowest band or its justification hedges — are routed to human review. The team calibrates the tone judge monthly against a 150-example sample stratified to include length variation and known-difficult cases, using an 80%-agreement threshold they set before the first calibration run. When agreement fell to 71% after a judge-model provider update, {% highlight %}they treated that as a signal to hold the judge score out of the next release gate{% /highlight %} until the rubric was tightened and recalibrated — not as an anomaly to explain away.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Decision room" title="The judge score moved. That's not the same as the product improving." body="Before trusting a rising helpfulness score, check whether the judge itself changed — a new provider version, a rephrased rubric, a different sampling rule — and re-run the calibration set. A score that moves for the wrong reason is worse than no score at all, because it looks like evidence." tone="paper" /%}

## Leave the reader with the next move

- [ ] Every criterion the team scores has been routed through the two questions to deterministic, judge, or human — not defaulted to "judge" because it's convenient.
- [ ] Every judge-scored criterion has a written rubric with named sub-criteria, a bounded scale, and a required justification field.
- [ ] A calibration sample exists with a documented size, selection method, and pre-set agreement threshold.
- [ ] Re-calibration is tied to judge-model, prompt, and rubric changes, not left to run only when something looks wrong.
- [ ] Borderline or low-confidence judge scores escalate to a human reviewer rather than resolving automatically.
- [ ] Nobody on the team is treating an uncalibrated judge score as ground truth in a release decision.

An LLM judge earns a place in an evaluation system the same way any other signal does: by being checked against something more trustworthy before it's trusted on its own, and by staying inside the criteria it's actually good at. {% highlight %}Route the easy checks to code, keep the accountable decisions with a person{% /highlight %}, and let the judge do the volume work in between — with a calibration process that keeps it honest as your model, prompt, and traffic all keep changing underneath it. [RAG evaluation](/blog/rag-evaluation-guide) covers a closely related case — judging groundedness and citation quality — where several of the same biases, especially verbosity and rubric drift, show up in a different shape.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="What is LLM-as-a-judge?" answer="Using one model to score another model's output against a defined rubric, typically for criteria too subjective or too expensive for a deterministic check." /%}
{% faq-item question="Is an LLM judge's score ground truth?" answer="No. It should be calibrated against human judgment on a representative sample and treated as an estimate, not an objective fact." /%}
{% faq-item question="What is position bias?" answer="A judge's tendency to favor whichever answer is presented first (or second) in a comparison regardless of quality, which is why answer order should be randomized during evaluation." /%}
{% faq-item question="What is verbosity bias?" answer="A judge's tendency to rate longer answers as better independent of their actual correctness or usefulness." /%}
{% faq-item question="When should a human review instead of a judge?" answer="When the criterion requires ground-truth fact-checking, high-stakes or irreversible judgment, or when judge-human agreement on a calibration sample falls below the threshold the team set in advance." /%}
